WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:04.000
The most profound discovery of our time&nbsp;
is that our universe had a beginning

00:00:04.000 --> 00:00:05.200
and that it’s expanding.

00:00:05.200 --> 00:00:09.040
Yet the person responsible for this&nbsp;
discovery is virtually unknown.

00:00:09.040 --> 00:00:12.080
And there’s a reason for&nbsp;
it, which will become clear.

00:00:12.080 --> 00:00:15.440
Georges Lemaître was a Belgian&nbsp;
priest who theorized that the

00:00:15.440 --> 00:00:18.320
expansion of the universe began with the explosion

00:00:18.320 --> 00:00:23.200
of a single particle at a definite point&nbsp;
in time faster than the speed of light.

00:00:23.200 --> 00:00:27.280
The Big Bang Theory is accepted&nbsp;
by nearly all astronomers today.

00:00:27.280 --> 00:00:30.160
But back in the 1930s, the belief that the entire

00:00:30.160 --> 00:00:34.640
observable universe began with a&nbsp;
bang was considered preposterous.

00:00:34.640 --> 00:00:37.120
Even Albert Einstein found it far-fetched.

00:00:37.120 --> 00:00:39.360
So how did Lemaître come up with this theory?

00:00:40.480 --> 00:00:42.080
Let’s go back to the beginning.

00:00:42.080 --> 00:00:49.040
Lemaître was born in Charleroi, Belgium in&nbsp;
1894 to a well-off, devoutly Catholic family.

00:00:49.040 --> 00:00:53.920
He knew what he wanted to be when he was nine&nbsp;
years old: both a priest and a scientist.

00:00:53.920 --> 00:00:57.360
He grew up in a coal-mining region&nbsp;
and at the insistence of his father,

00:00:57.360 --> 00:01:02.640
studied civil mining engineering at the Catholic&nbsp;
research university in the Belgian city of Louvain

00:01:02.640 --> 00:01:07.440
only to have his studies interrupted&nbsp;
when Germany invaded Belgium in 1914.

00:01:07.440 --> 00:01:10.480
He left university to fight&nbsp;
as an artillery officer

00:01:10.480 --> 00:01:13.920
and received the Belgian War Cross&nbsp;
for bravery on the battlefield.

00:01:13.920 --> 00:01:16.080
At this time, he was already thinking about the

00:01:16.080 --> 00:01:19.600
origin of the universe in the&nbsp;
context of his Christian faith.

00:01:19.600 --> 00:01:22.480
He wrote to a friend while in the trenches of war:

00:01:22.480 --> 00:01:26.160
“I have understood the ‘Fiat Lux’&nbsp;
as the reason of the universe.”

00:01:26.160 --> 00:01:30.320
“Fiat looks” is Latin for “let there be&nbsp;
light”, in reference to the third verse

00:01:30.320 --> 00:01:34.800
in the Book of Genesis: "God said, 'Let&nbsp;
there be light,' and there was light".

00:01:34.800 --> 00:01:37.200
After the war, he continued with his education but

00:01:37.200 --> 00:01:41.120
switched to mathematics and&nbsp;
physics, graduating in 1920.

00:01:41.120 --> 00:01:45.600
Then he went to seminary school and became&nbsp;
ordained as a priest three years later.

00:01:45.600 --> 00:01:50.240
During this time, he became fascinated by&nbsp;
Einstein’s General Theory of Relativity - how

00:01:50.240 --> 00:01:54.560
gravity can warp space and time, which&nbsp;
I talked about in a previous video.

00:01:54.560 --> 00:01:58.080
Lemaître then did post-graduate&nbsp;
work at the University of Cambridge

00:01:58.080 --> 00:02:01.760
under famed English astronomer Sir&nbsp;
Arthur Eddington, who had confirmed

00:02:01.760 --> 00:02:06.720
Einstein’s theory when he saw how gravity&nbsp;
bent light from a star traveling near the sun.

00:02:06.720 --> 00:02:10.160
Eddington gave Lemaître a glowing&nbsp;
recommendation after which he spent

00:02:10.160 --> 00:02:14.640
time at Harvard and then got accepted&nbsp;
into a doctoral program at MIT.

00:02:14.640 --> 00:02:18.000
It was a good time to be in the U.S. if&nbsp;
you were a scientist because of recent

00:02:18.000 --> 00:02:21.680
discoveries that shook peoples’&nbsp;
understanding of the universe.

00:02:21.680 --> 00:02:24.160
Up until then, the Milky Way - which includes our

00:02:24.160 --> 00:02:27.280
solar system - was thought&nbsp;
to be the entire universe.

00:02:27.280 --> 00:02:30.560
But more powerful telescopes&nbsp;
showed this wasn’t true.

00:02:30.560 --> 00:02:33.040
American astronomer Edwin Hubble announced he

00:02:33.040 --> 00:02:37.200
had evidence the Milky Way was one&nbsp;
of many galaxies in the universe.

00:02:37.200 --> 00:02:41.120
He observed that the spiral-shaped&nbsp;
Andromeda was in fact a galaxy.

00:02:41.120 --> 00:02:43.680
Another American astronomer, Vesto Slipher,

00:02:43.680 --> 00:02:48.000
noticed Andromeda was bluer in color&nbsp;
whereas other galaxies were redder.

00:02:48.000 --> 00:02:52.000
Slipher knew Andromeda was approaching&nbsp;
us because if light is coming toward you,

00:02:52.000 --> 00:02:55.920
the waves get more squished, making the&nbsp;
light bluer. If it goes away from you,

00:02:55.920 --> 00:02:59.120
it gets stretched out, making&nbsp;
the light waves appear redder.

00:02:59.120 --> 00:03:02.320
The light of distant galaxies that&nbsp;
appeared to shift more toward the

00:03:02.320 --> 00:03:06.640
red end of the color spectrum suggested&nbsp;
they were getting farther away from Earth.

00:03:06.640 --> 00:03:11.040
The belief was that the galaxies were&nbsp;
moving away in a static universe.

00:03:11.040 --> 00:03:14.320
However, Lemaître interpreted&nbsp;
this redshift differently.

00:03:14.320 --> 00:03:18.720
He concluded that the galaxies were not moving&nbsp;
away from each other in a static universe,

00:03:18.720 --> 00:03:23.680
but rather, the universe itself was expanding,&nbsp;
which then stretched out the lightwaves.

00:03:23.680 --> 00:03:25.280
Here’s another way to look at it.

00:03:25.280 --> 00:03:27.600
Imagine that this balloon is the universe.

00:03:27.600 --> 00:03:29.360
And the dots the galaxies.

00:03:29.360 --> 00:03:34.080
As the balloon expands, the separation between&nbsp;
the dots also expands. And from the perspective

00:03:34.080 --> 00:03:39.200
of each individual dot, or galaxy, it appears&nbsp;
that the others are moving farther away from it.

00:03:39.200 --> 00:03:42.320
An expanding universe completely&nbsp;
shattered the notion that the

00:03:42.320 --> 00:03:45.840
universe was static - which is what&nbsp;
Einstein strongly believed. But an

00:03:45.840 --> 00:03:49.760
unchanging universe was problematic&nbsp;
for his own theory of relativity.

00:03:49.760 --> 00:03:53.120
If gravity were the only active&nbsp;
force in a static universe,

00:03:53.120 --> 00:03:56.880
the universe would collapse. To get&nbsp;
around this issue, Einstein added a

00:03:56.880 --> 00:04:02.240
cosmological constant - presumably a form&nbsp;
of matter or energy that opposes gravity.

00:04:02.240 --> 00:04:07.520
On the other hand, Lemaître’s expanding universe&nbsp;
would satisfy Einstein’s theory of relativity.

00:04:07.520 --> 00:04:12.800
Still, Einstein wasn’t swayed. He refused to&nbsp;
believe in an expanding universe. Lemaître

00:04:12.800 --> 00:04:18.560
recalled Einstein told him: “Your calculations&nbsp;
are correct, but your physics is atrocious".

00:04:18.560 --> 00:04:21.680
It would take Einstein a long time to come around.

00:04:21.680 --> 00:04:27.760
He did finally abandon his cosmological constant,&nbsp;
calling it the “greatest blunder” of his life.

00:04:27.760 --> 00:04:31.680
Lemaître returned to Europe in 1925&nbsp;
to take up a teaching post at the

00:04:31.680 --> 00:04:35.280
Catholic University of Louvain&nbsp;
where he was formerly a student.

00:04:35.280 --> 00:04:39.280
In 1927 he published his case&nbsp;
for an expanding universe

00:04:39.280 --> 00:04:42.080
in French in a Belgian scientific journal.

00:04:42.080 --> 00:04:46.720
This should have rocked the world. But it&nbsp;
didn’t. No one really paid any attention to it.

00:04:46.720 --> 00:04:50.240
It wasn’t until two years later when&nbsp;
Hubble published his own findings about

00:04:50.240 --> 00:04:53.280
an expanding universe that the world took notice.

00:04:53.280 --> 00:04:55.680
Hubble measured the distance of galaxies and also

00:04:55.680 --> 00:04:58.560
built on the work of Slipher&nbsp;
who had measured redshifts.

00:04:58.560 --> 00:05:03.360
He plotted the distance of galaxies and their&nbsp;
velocities in this classic graph and showed

00:05:03.360 --> 00:05:08.720
that more distant galaxies were moving away&nbsp;
from us faster than galaxies that were closer.

00:05:08.720 --> 00:05:11.600
And it made sense if the universe were expanding.

00:05:11.600 --> 00:05:13.600
This became known as Hubble’s law.

00:05:13.600 --> 00:05:16.000
He came to the same conclusion as Lemaître.

00:05:16.000 --> 00:05:19.520
Yet Lemaître didn’t get any of the&nbsp;
attention and accolades bestowed

00:05:19.520 --> 00:05:23.040
on Hubble because Lemaître had&nbsp;
published his findings in French

00:05:23.040 --> 00:05:27.600
in an obscure Belgian journal that wasn’t&nbsp;
widely read outside of his own country.

00:05:27.600 --> 00:05:30.720
His mentor Eddington is said to&nbsp;
have helped arrange an English

00:05:30.720 --> 00:05:35.440
translation two years after the&nbsp;
release of Hubble’s work, in 1931.

00:05:35.440 --> 00:05:40.000
However, the paper omitted Lemaître’s&nbsp;
crucial paragraphs describing Hubble’s law.

00:05:40.000 --> 00:05:43.760
Turns out, Lemaître chose to leave&nbsp;
this out himself. He reasoned that

00:05:43.760 --> 00:05:47.360
Hubble’s calculations had already&nbsp;
improved on his earlier work.

00:05:47.360 --> 00:05:51.760
He apparently wasn’t fussed at proving that he had&nbsp;
come up with the expansion theory before Hubble.

00:05:51.760 --> 00:05:56.000
He was a humble man who didn’t&nbsp;
mind that Hubble got the glory.

00:05:56.000 --> 00:06:00.160
Instead, Lemaître moved forward with&nbsp;
his research and published a new paper

00:06:00.160 --> 00:06:06.640
in 1931 in which he argued that if the universe&nbsp;
were expanding then it also had a beginning.

00:06:06.640 --> 00:06:10.000
He proposed this in his&nbsp;
hypothesis of the primeval atom.

00:06:10.000 --> 00:06:12.880
A primeval atom was a single particle.

00:06:12.880 --> 00:06:16.560
He argued that the universe began with&nbsp;
the explosion of this single particle

00:06:16.560 --> 00:06:22.560
that gave birth to our entire cosmos and&nbsp;
concluded a creation-like event had occurred.

00:06:22.560 --> 00:06:27.120
Researchers date the event to&nbsp;
more than 13.8 billion years ago.

00:06:27.120 --> 00:06:29.840
He didn’t call it the Big&nbsp;
Bang. Astronomer Sir Fred

00:06:29.840 --> 00:06:32.960
Hoyle coined the term during&nbsp;
a talk broadcast on the BBC.

00:06:32.960 --> 00:06:37.120
Hoyle actually thought the theory was&nbsp;
nonsense. Many scientists were skeptical,

00:06:37.120 --> 00:06:38.080
including Einstein.

00:06:38.080 --> 00:06:43.342
However, in 1933, when Lemaître and Einstein&nbsp;
traveled together to attend a series of

00:06:43.342 --> 00:06:47.708
seminars in California, and Lemaître had&nbsp;
time to explain his theory in detail,

00:06:47.708 --> 00:06:49.715
Einstein is purported to have said: “This is the

00:06:49.715 --> 00:06:55.585
most beautiful and satisfactory explanation&nbsp;
of creation to which I have ever listened.”

00:06:55.585 --> 00:07:00.000
Lemaître was nominated for the&nbsp;
Nobel Prize in Physics in 1954

00:07:00.000 --> 00:07:03.614
for his prediction of the expanding&nbsp;
universe. Two years later - he was

00:07:03.614 --> 00:07:08.644
nominated for the Nobel Prize in Chemistry for&nbsp;
his primeval atom theory. He didn’t win either.

00:07:08.644 --> 00:07:12.582
The strongest evidence supporting the&nbsp;
Big Bang was discovered by accident,

00:07:12.582 --> 00:07:19.376
a decade later, in 1964, by American scientists&nbsp;
Arno Penzias and Robert Wilson who were working

00:07:19.376 --> 00:07:23.827
for Bell Laboratories, the research and&nbsp;
development arm of what is today Nokia.

00:07:23.827 --> 00:07:29.916
They were baffled by a strange signal noise picked up by&nbsp;
this antenna and tried to figure out what it was.

00:07:29.916 --> 00:07:32.960
They even removed some pigeons that were&nbsp;
nesting there. That’s when they stumbled upon

00:07:32.960 --> 00:07:40.051
cosmic microwave background radiation. Ancient&nbsp;
light thought to be left over from the Big Bang.

00:07:40.051 --> 00:07:45.684
Lemaître lived to see the moment of their&nbsp;
discovery. His ideas were finally vindicated.

00:07:45.684 --> 00:07:51.265
He died two years later, in 1966,&nbsp;
from leukemia at the age of 71.

00:07:51.265 --> 00:07:55.276
Despite not being as arguably well&nbsp;
known as his fellow scientists,

00:07:55.276 --> 00:07:57.309
he is finally getting more recognition.

00:07:57.309 --> 00:08:02.065
In 2018, the Hubble law was&nbsp;
changed to the Hubble-Lemaître law.

00:08:02.065 --> 00:08:05.265
Lemaître may have helped uncover&nbsp;
the secrets of the universe.

00:08:05.265 --> 00:08:08.273
Yet the more we learn, the stranger it gets.

00:08:08.273 --> 00:08:12.540
Scientists thought that the expansion of&nbsp;
the universe would gradually slow down over

00:08:12.540 --> 00:08:16.079
time as gravity put on the brakes. But surprisingly,

00:08:16.079 --> 00:08:20.146
they found the opposite. Turns out the&nbsp;
expansion of the universe is accelerating.

00:08:20.146 --> 00:08:25.105
Some mysterious force is driving our&nbsp;
universe apart. And we don’t know why.

00:08:25.105 --> 00:08:29.425
Perhaps in time, this mystery will also be solved.

